1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:10,743
Welcome to Comb Circuit Live, version zero point two. This walkthrough explains the live simulator, its controls, every adjustable parameter, and how to

2
00:00:10,743 --> 00:00:12,079
interpret your experiments.

3
00:00:12,329 --> 00:00:21,865
The old explorer replayed saved curves. This version calculates new voltages as you run it, including responses to inputs and parameter changes you choose.

4
00:00:22,115 --> 00:00:31,548
Unzip the project and open LIVE dot H T M L in a browser. No account, Python installation, or internet connection is needed to use the simulator.

5
00:00:31,798 --> 00:00:40,453
The anatomy is measured, but the electrical properties are hypotheses. Live computation does not mean we have recorded activity from a living animal.

6
00:00:41,767 --> 00:00:50,896
The organism is the comb jelly Mnemiopsis leidyi. The selected circuit comes from its aboral organ, which is associated with balance and ciliary

7
00:00:50,896 --> 00:00:51,293
coordination.

8
00:00:51,543 --> 00:01:01,169
The reconstruction contains three aboral nerve net neurons, abbreviated A N N, twenty six bridge cells, and one hundred twenty nine balancer cells.

9
00:01:01,419 --> 00:01:10,652
That is one hundred fifty eight cells, including non-neuronal balancer cells, with two hundred eighty directed anatomical contacts. It is not the whole

10
00:01:10,652 --> 00:01:11,053
animal.

11
00:01:11,303 --> 00:01:20,123
The original anatomical work belongs to Jokura and colleagues. Our contribution is an exploratory electrical modeling workbench built on their data.

12
00:01:21,433 --> 00:01:30,968
The branching picture shows only the three reconstructed A N N arbors. Q one through four spans all four anatomical quadrants; the other two arbors each

13
00:01:30,968 --> 00:01:32,069
span two quadrants.

14
00:01:32,319 --> 00:01:41,274
These neurons have syncytial morphology, including continuous branched structures and multiple nuclei. The visible lines are traced skeletons, not

15
00:01:41,274 --> 00:01:43,159
thousands of separate neurons.

16
00:01:43,409 --> 00:01:52,902
The spatial model divides the arbors into sixteen thousand four hundred forty six electrical compartments. Bridge and balancer cells each use one

17
00:01:52,902 --> 00:01:56,353
compartment, giving sixteen thousand six hundred one altogether.

18
00:01:56,603 --> 00:02:06,399
Line crossings in a screen projection are not proof of a connection. The exported skeleton trees may also omit internal reconnecting loops, so cuts must be

19
00:02:06,399 --> 00:02:07,153
interpreted cautiously.

20
00:02:08,433 --> 00:02:17,245
The spatial model allows voltage to differ between locations within one A N N neuron. Current spreads through its reconstructed internal paths and leaks

21
00:02:17,245 --> 00:02:18,346
through its membrane.

22
00:02:18,596 --> 00:02:27,302
The point model, also called equipotential, gives each cell just one voltage. It cannot represent a local gradient within that cell.

23
00:02:27,552 --> 00:02:37,106
Both models receive the same injected current and anatomical contacts, and have matched total cell capacitance and leak. Solid graph lines show the spatial

24
00:02:37,106 --> 00:02:39,893
model; dashed lines show the point model.

25
00:02:40,143 --> 00:02:49,252
A local probe can disagree strongly between models even when the whole-cell averages look similar. More anatomical detail alone does not establish which

26
00:02:49,252 --> 00:02:50,836
response is biologically correct.

27
00:02:52,100 --> 00:03:01,628
Press Run to begin, and press Pause to stop at the current state. Step advances ten milliseconds of simulated time without starting continuous playback.

28
00:03:01,878 --> 00:03:10,879
With the demo option checked, a one picoampere pulse is scheduled from twenty to forty milliseconds after a reset. The pulse uses the currently chosen

29
00:03:10,879 --> 00:03:12,679
input site and pulse settings.

30
00:03:12,929 --> 00:03:22,305
The speed selector controls requested simulated time per wall-clock second. One times biological time requests one thousand simulated milliseconds each

31
00:03:22,305 --> 00:03:25,587
second, but slower computers may achieve less.

32
00:03:25,837 --> 00:03:36,785
The clock always shows simulated time. The solver does not skip numerical steps to catch up. Reset clears voltages, time, trace history and the event log,

33
00:03:36,785 --> 00:03:39,732
while keeping your current probes and settings.

34
00:03:41,033 --> 00:03:50,124
Set Click action to Add local probe, then click a branch. A colored ring marks the selected compartment, and a matching trace appears in the measurement

35
00:03:50,124 --> 00:03:50,474
panel.

36
00:03:50,724 --> 00:03:59,683
The click selects a nearby traced compartment in the current screen projection. Rotate or zoom to separate overlapping branches before choosing a location.

37
00:03:59,933 --> 00:04:09,954
You can display up to twelve arbitrarily chosen probes together. Remove one with its cross button to free a channel. The limit is for readable display, not

38
00:04:09,954 --> 00:04:11,439
six fixed biological measurements.

39
00:04:11,689 --> 00:04:20,139
Adding or removing a probe starts a new trace window without resetting the electrical state. Newly added probes cannot show activity that was never

40
00:04:20,139 --> 00:04:21,195
recorded for them.

41
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:32,031
The Any cell menu includes all one hundred fifty eight cells. Choose an individual bridge or balancer cell and press Add cell mean to inspect its response.

42
00:04:32,281 --> 00:04:41,560
For an A N N cell, the mean averages its compartments with weights proportional to capacitance. Larger membrane contributions count more than small pieces.

43
00:04:41,810 --> 00:04:51,322
The group menu adds means across bridge cells, balancer cells, or all A N N neurons. A local node probe instead reads one specific compartment.

44
00:04:51,572 --> 00:05:00,948
You can also enter an original A N N treenode identifier. In the point comparison, every local probe within a particular cell necessarily reads the same

45
00:05:00,948 --> 00:05:01,670
cell voltage.

46
00:05:02,967 --> 00:05:12,906
Set Click action to Move injection site and click a branch. The white ring moves to that location. Enter a current amplitude and pulse length, then press

47
00:05:12,906 --> 00:05:13,642
Inject now.

48
00:05:13,892 --> 00:05:23,617
The pulse begins at the next simulated step. If paused, use Run or Step to advance through it. Pulse length is rounded to the fixed half-millisecond

49
00:05:23,617 --> 00:05:23,991
timestep.

50
00:05:24,241 --> 00:05:33,967
Positive and negative pulses are allowed. Larger magnitude usually gives a stronger response, while longer pulses supply current for longer. Repeated or

51
00:05:33,967 --> 00:05:35,735
overlapping pulses can interact.

52
00:05:35,985 --> 00:05:45,899
The Inject into selected cell shortcut also allows bridge or balancer stimulation. For an A N N cell it selects the first exported compartment, not an

53
00:05:45,899 --> 00:05:49,711
identified soma, so a branch click gives more precise control.

54
00:05:50,967 --> 00:05:59,528
Voltage means deviation from a resting reference. Zero millivolts means at that reference, not a biological absolute membrane voltage of zero.

55
00:05:59,778 --> 00:06:09,483
In voltage color mode, blue is negative, muted teal is near rest, and gold is positive. The chosen plus-or-minus range controls the display; values beyond

56
00:06:09,483 --> 00:06:11,813
it clip to the endpoint color.

57
00:06:12,063 --> 00:06:21,172
The time plot uses milliseconds horizontally and millivolts vertically. All selected probes share one automatically scaled vertical axis, so large

58
00:06:21,172 --> 00:06:23,905
responses may visually hide small ones.

59
00:06:24,155 --> 00:06:34,542
The table gives the latest numerical readings. The window selector changes how much recent time is visible. These voltages are not spike rates, calcium

60
00:06:34,542 --> 00:06:37,571
fluorescence, ciliary beat frequencies, or swimming predictions.

61
00:06:38,833 --> 00:06:48,021
Cable radius defaults to zero point two five micrometers everywhere along the A N N arbors. This uniform radius is assumed, not a measured diameter

62
00:06:48,021 --> 00:06:48,389
profile.

63
00:06:48,639 --> 00:06:58,218
Increasing radius increases membrane area, capacitance, and total leak at fixed time constant. It also increases internal cable conductance with the square

64
00:06:58,218 --> 00:06:59,089
of radius.

65
00:06:59,339 --> 00:07:08,287
Doubling radius therefore doubles capacitance but quadruples internal conductance. Current can spread more effectively, but the same input is distributed

66
00:07:08,287 --> 00:07:09,630
across more membrane.

67
00:07:09,880 --> 00:07:19,347
The resulting voltage is not guaranteed to increase. Radius changes geometry-dependent electrical load and coupling together, so compare several probes and

68
00:07:19,347 --> 00:07:21,602
use resets for controlled trials.

69
00:07:22,900 --> 00:07:31,630
Axial resistivity defaults to one hundred fifty ohm centimeters. It represents how difficult it is for current to flow through the internal material.

70
00:07:31,880 --> 00:07:40,876
Increasing resistivity weakens coupling between neighboring compartments. Decreasing it strengthens that coupling and tends to reduce spatial voltage

71
00:07:40,876 --> 00:07:41,376
differences.

72
00:07:41,626 --> 00:07:50,946
The internal conductance multiplier defaults to one and changes coupling directly without adding membrane area. Zero removes all internal cable coupling,

73
00:07:50,946 --> 00:07:52,721
an extreme mathematical scenario.

74
00:07:52,971 --> 00:08:01,801
With sufficiently strong internal coupling, the spatial model approaches the point model. This limiting behavior is one of the implementation checks, not

75
00:08:01,801 --> 00:08:04,611
evidence that the chosen default is physiological.

76
00:08:05,900 --> 00:08:15,490
Capacitance describes charge storage per voltage change. Specific capacitance defaults to one microfarad per square centimeter for the A N N membrane.

77
00:08:15,740 --> 00:08:24,869
Bridge and balancer cells each have an assumed capacitance of zero point five picofarads. Increasing capacitance generally reduces voltage response to a

78
00:08:24,869 --> 00:08:25,699
fixed current.

79
00:08:25,949 --> 00:08:35,234
The leak time constant defaults to twenty milliseconds. A larger value means weaker leak at fixed capacitance, allowing responses to persist longer.

80
00:08:35,484 --> 00:08:44,837
In this code leak conductance equals capacitance divided by the time constant. Changing capacitance while keeping the time constant fixed also changes

81
00:08:44,837 --> 00:08:47,812
leak. You are not varying capacitance alone.

82
00:08:49,100 --> 00:08:58,822
Each anatomical contact contributes a current determined by its presynaptic voltage. The current scale defaults to zero point zero five picoamperes per

83
00:08:58,822 --> 00:08:59,264
contact.

84
00:08:59,514 --> 00:09:09,380
This is a saturating scale, not a constant current delivered at every moment. The model uses a hyperbolic tangent of presynaptic voltage divided by a five

85
00:09:09,380 --> 00:09:10,139
millivolt scale.

86
00:09:10,389 --> 00:09:20,358
Increasing current scale strengthens contact effects. Setting it to zero switches off synaptic currents. Increasing the voltage scale makes contacts less

87
00:09:20,358 --> 00:09:22,731
sensitive to small voltage changes.

88
00:09:22,981 --> 00:09:32,307
Every contact is assigned equal strength in this release. Multiple contacts add contributions, but anatomical contact count is not a measured functional

89
00:09:32,307 --> 00:09:32,731
weight.

90
00:09:34,033 --> 00:09:43,956
The sign setting applies uniformly to every contact. Positive sign makes positive presynaptic deviations generate positive current; negative sign reverses

91
00:09:43,956 --> 00:09:44,948
that relationship.

92
00:09:45,198 --> 00:09:54,458
This signed approximation does not identify neurotransmitters or reproduce receptor kinetics. Negative input and feedback loops can make the network

93
00:09:54,458 --> 00:09:58,162
response more complicated than simply excitation versus inhibition.

94
00:09:58,412 --> 00:10:06,017
Turning off bridge-to-A N N feedback disables forty four contacts. It does not remove bridge cells or their other connections.

95
00:10:06,267 --> 00:10:15,783
Press Apply parameters now to activate changed settings. Existing voltages are retained and the change is logged. To compare from the same resting state,

96
00:10:15,783 --> 00:10:17,369
reset before each trial.

97
00:10:18,633 --> 00:10:27,857
Set Click action to Select cable cut and click a branch. A gold cross marks the disconnected internal edge. Selecting a different edge restores the

98
00:10:27,857 --> 00:10:28,595
previous one.

99
00:10:28,845 --> 00:10:38,452
You can instead choose the balanced default cut, then press Apply. That preset approximately divides the central arbor into two similarly sized portions of

100
00:10:38,452 --> 00:10:39,653
traced cable length.

101
00:10:39,903 --> 00:10:49,744
The cut retains membrane and synaptic contacts on both sides. It models neither cell death nor wound healing, and other cells can still provide indirect

102
00:10:49,744 --> 00:10:51,712
paths between the separated regions.

103
00:10:51,962 --> 00:11:01,384
The point comparison cannot represent a local cable cut, so it remains internally equipotential. A local voltage can increase after a cut because there are

104
00:11:01,384 --> 00:11:04,399
fewer internal paths for injected current to spread.

105
00:11:05,700 --> 00:11:15,085
For a first experiment, choose two distant sites on the central A N N and add local probes. Also add that cell mean and one balancer-cell readout.

106
00:11:15,335 --> 00:11:24,101
Reset, inject a pulse at one site, and compare local responses with the cell mean. Then export your results before changing the probe set.

107
00:11:24,351 --> 00:11:34,750
Change one parameter, such as axial resistivity, reset again, and repeat the same input. Keep the sites, pulse amplitude, pulse duration, and other

108
00:11:34,750 --> 00:11:35,654
parameters fixed.

109
00:11:35,904 --> 00:11:44,607
Look for differences in peak amplitude, timing, and decay. A reproducible model difference suggests a useful measurement to investigate; it does not yet

110
00:11:44,607 --> 00:11:46,877
prove what the real animal does.

111
00:11:48,167 --> 00:11:58,146
Export traces C S V saves the selected probes for both models at each retained half-millisecond time step. The browser retains up to ten thousand recent

112
00:11:58,146 --> 00:12:01,216
samples, which is five seconds of simulated time.

113
00:12:01,466 --> 00:12:12,447
Export experiment J S O N saves initial and current settings, interventions, probe-set history, and the retained traces. It is an experiment log, not a

114
00:12:12,447 --> 00:12:13,765
resumable full-state checkpoint.

115
00:12:14,015 --> 00:12:21,904
Export before resetting, clearing the window, or changing probes. The plot window does not change the underlying retention limit.

116
00:12:22,154 --> 00:12:31,970
The project also includes original source files, provenance hashes, the Python reference, and numerical tests. Keep these with your exports so the

117
00:12:31,970 --> 00:12:34,647
anatomical and mathematical assumptions remain traceable.

118
00:12:35,900 --> 00:12:44,721
The earlier E X P L O R E page remains in the package as a fixed reference. Its wiring matrix uses rows as senders and columns as receivers.

119
00:12:44,971 --> 00:12:54,916
An entry counts contacts, not neurons, spikes, or measured synaptic strength. That anatomical matrix does not change when you disable feedback in a

120
00:12:54,916 --> 00:12:55,349
simulation.

121
00:12:55,599 --> 00:13:05,911
The saved comparison image shows an x y projection of the three arbors, then central A N N mean voltage and balancer mean voltage over time. The time plots

122
00:13:05,911 --> 00:13:07,333
use different vertical scales.

123
00:13:07,583 --> 00:13:16,190
The original eighteen-scenario sensitivity table compares radii, signs and current scales. Its root mean squared difference measures disagreement between

124
00:13:16,190 --> 00:13:20,719
models, not error against experimental truth or a confidence interval.

125
00:13:22,033 --> 00:13:31,378
The live solver uses a direct tree solution for passive membrane and internal currents, with synaptic currents evaluated from the previous time step. The

126
00:13:31,378 --> 00:13:34,882
live timestep is fixed at zero point five milliseconds.

127
00:13:35,132 --> 00:13:44,124
There is no longer a fixed one hundred sixty millisecond endpoint. The simulation continues until you pause or reset, and switching away from the tab

128
00:13:44,124 --> 00:13:44,843
pauses it.

129
00:13:45,093 --> 00:13:55,009
The JavaScript solver was compared with the Python SciPy reference across full compartment states, perturbations, and parameter changes. Their close

130
00:13:55,009 --> 00:13:56,992
agreement checks implementation consistency.

131
00:13:57,242 --> 00:14:06,434
Extreme parameters and long runs still require convergence checks and biological constraints. The next scientific goal is a prediction that survives

132
00:14:06,434 --> 00:14:10,811
plausible assumptions and can be compared with an independent experiment.
